olor:#121212;"><p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">5 October is a date notable=
 for two extraordinary goalscoring feats. Although Len Shackleton, the cont=
roversial inside-forward, would later fall out with Newcastle and join thei=
r closest rivals Sunderland, there was no hint of the acrimony to come when=
 he made his debut in 1946 after a move from Bradford Park Avenue. Shacklet=
on scored six, still a club record, three of them in a 150-second burst, as=
 Newcastle recorded their largest-ever victory, beating Newport 13-0. They =
hadn=E2=80=99t won any of the four games before that, though, and didn=E2=
=80=99t win either of the two after. Newcastle finished fifth that season, =
10 points off the promotion places to the top tier, while Shackleton racked=
 up a total of 19 goals to go with the four he had already scored for Bradf=
ord Park Avenue. Newport, unsurprisingly, were relegated, bottom of the tab=
le.<br><br>Forty-seven years later, Robbie Fowler, who had made his debut j=
ust a month earlier, scored all five goals as Liverpool beat Fulham 5-0, to=
 complete an 8-1 aggregate win in the second round of the League Cup. Liver=
pool beat Ipswich in the following round but then went out on penalties to =
Wimbledon in a fourth-round replay.</p></div></td></tr>
